WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.910
Welcome to Ear Biscuits,

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:05.859
the podcast where two lifelong friends

00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.510
talk about life for a long time.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:10.343
I'm Link.

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:11.176
And I'm Rhett.

00:00:11.176 --> 00:00:13.060
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.500
we're going to be doing
a couple of things.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:16.900
One, we're gonna be exploring

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.600
a internet phenomenon

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.470
that I already know that most
of you are familiar with,

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.270
but we're gonna be breaking it down.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:28.470
And we're going to be revealing a secret

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.990
that I don't think that
we've revealed before,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.170
about a video that we
made a long time ago.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:38.170
We're coming clean about
something dishonest

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.153
that we did for a video.

00:00:42.287 --> 00:00:44.171
And we tend to be honest.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:46.720
We tend to try to be honest.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.400
I think this is going to be good,

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.080
for, to get this off my chest.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:52.913
I can't.

00:00:52.913 --> 00:00:54.984
I mean, if it does come up,

00:00:54.984 --> 00:00:57.063
it pops up in my brain.

00:00:58.041 --> 00:00:58.874
A little shame.

00:00:58.874 --> 00:01:00.171
There's a little shame,

00:01:00.171 --> 00:01:05.171
because of it not being discussed.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.530
So, we're going to bring
it out into the open.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.170
I will say, just in case
we're doing something

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:10.088
that we often do,

00:01:10.088 --> 00:01:11.926
which is we think that
we're saying something

00:01:11.926 --> 00:01:12.870
for the first time.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.536
And we actually, "Oh
guys, like five years ago,

00:01:14.536 --> 00:01:16.442
"You said this on Ear Biscuits."

00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:19.590
Our memories, it isn't
that our memories are bad.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:24.590
It's that you essentially
have just an open channel

00:01:24.713 --> 00:01:27.070
directly to all our experiences,

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.890
because we talk about
everything that we do and think.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:30.750
And so--

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.450
There's a difference between
remembering something

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.030
and remembering if you've talked about it.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:34.863
Right.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.610
So, we apologize if we've
already come clean about this.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:39.932
And I don't think, I mean,

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:43.470
there are certain people that
have been a part of my life

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.815
through the years who,
they've been, I forgot,

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:48.840
they've forgotten that
they've told me this.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.290
So, they're telling me this
and the exact same way.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.460
I mean, my mom does that a lot now.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.150
Yeah, that happens as they age.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:56.855
I was talking to her this morning,

00:01:56.855 --> 00:02:01.855
she had her second cataract surgery.

00:02:03.968 --> 00:02:05.410
And this was the seventh time

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.008
she's told you about the
second cataract surgery.

00:02:08.008 --> 00:02:11.517
Well, she's told me about
five different times

00:02:11.517 --> 00:02:13.499
even though her right eye,

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:15.483
there's something else wrong with it

00:02:15.483 --> 00:02:17.630
that makes it hard for her to see out of,

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:19.610
they've decided to do
the surgery, you know,

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.390
to remove the cataract in that eye anyway,

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:25.019
because who knows in the future, you know,

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:27.720
there might be something else
that can fix that problem.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.610
And then the cataract
will already be removed.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.040
And you never know if it will increase

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:37.040
her range of vision in that eye anyway.

00:02:37.201 --> 00:02:39.660
And what I just told you,

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.820
she has told me for the
first time, five times.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.673
And it's not, I don't, I
mean, maybe it's an age--

00:02:45.673 --> 00:02:46.770
it's what she's thinking about.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.230
Maybe it's an age thing, but it's like,

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:50.377
it's the one thing that when
you start talking about,

00:02:50.377 --> 00:02:51.737
"Well I had this done to my eye

00:02:51.737 --> 00:02:54.150
"and I'm getting it done to my other eye."

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.230
It's just the thing that you talk about.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.127
And it's, and when you tell
everybody that you talk about,

00:02:59.127 --> 00:03:01.590
"Well you know, I'm having
this procedure done."

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:02.770
It's part of it.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:06.050
And then, I mean, I guess it's
a good sign that I've talked

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:08.850
to her enough that I've heard
the same thing five times

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:10.620
because you know, I've been
talking to her about her.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.980
I good news is, she's got her second.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.770
She's got the second eye done.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:16.430
And everything went well.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:20.886
She, we still don't know
because it was yesterday.

00:03:20.886 --> 00:03:24.930
If she's realizing any of
the benefits or who knows

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:29.192
maybe in the future as
technology develops, you know

00:03:29.192 --> 00:03:31.290
there'll be something that
can fix the other problem.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.900
And then the cataract
would already be removed.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.566
I don't know if I said that.
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:34.566 --> 00:03:35.399
I like that.

00:03:35.399 --> 00:03:36.232
That's good.

00:03:36.232 --> 00:03:37.500
That was, that was a nice reference.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.333
So yeah.

00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:42.320
Do you correct her, do
you correct her when she?

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:43.153
You know what--

00:03:43.153 --> 00:03:44.247
Yeah, you told me that.

00:03:44.247 --> 00:03:45.760
The second time I was like, yeah

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.276
you told me that, third time

00:03:48.276 --> 00:03:50.020
I don't think I said anything.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.500
Oh wow, I think you got
to say it every time.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:53.333
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:54.166
You told me that.

00:03:54.166 --> 00:03:55.380
Well, it's just good to--

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:56.550
What's the update?

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:57.597
It's just good to have a conversation.

00:03:57.597 --> 00:03:58.900
What's the latest?

00:03:58.900 --> 00:03:59.733
You know?

00:03:59.733 --> 00:04:03.070
I think success is just having
a conversation where she's

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:07.210
you know, she's processing
and you know, I'm trying,

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:09.600
I try to ask questions that
will get a different answer.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.890
Yeah, well, speaking of old
people with bad memories

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.292
I was on Facebook recently.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:16.823
That's nice.

00:04:18.657 --> 00:04:22.150
And this is a choice,
because this is not a habit.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.780
I mean, I'll find myself on Twitter

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:27.890
and I didn't even
realize it was happening,

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.370
but that never happens with Facebook.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:30.952
I do not go on Facebook.

00:04:30.952 --> 00:04:33.285
I don't have it on my phone.

00:04:34.237 --> 00:04:35.877
Do you?

00:04:35.877 --> 00:04:37.430
I have it on my phone

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:42.273
and the reason that, I had
it on my phone for years,

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.483
but I do not, I'm not a Facebook user,

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.033
it sounds like a drug.

00:04:50.587 --> 00:04:51.640
You can make that argument.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.563
I do not participate, however,

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:59.270
recently when I was trying to
contact people to be a part

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:04.210
of Jesse's birthday video,
I was spamming like family

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.267
and friends and old friends.

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:07.470
Got to go to Facebook.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.250
The only way I can get in
touch with some of her old

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:10.463
friends was Facebook.

00:05:12.394 --> 00:05:16.330
One of the reasons I do
go to Facebook at times,

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:17.650
and I don't know what this says about me,

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.785
but there's a couple of people,

00:05:20.785 --> 00:05:23.820
first of all, if you do go back in time

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.890
to when we were first getting on Facebook,

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.410
of course, we were individuals on Facebook

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.180
and before, you could
have like a Facebook page

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:34.570
that was a brand, the Rhett &amp; Link page,

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:36.970
the Mythical page that we have now,

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:40.960
we basically used our
personal Facebook pages

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.445
for promotional purposes, right?

00:05:42.445 --> 00:05:45.034
During the early days,
writing the Facebook song,

00:05:45.034 --> 00:05:46.585
posting that, that kind of thing.

00:05:46.585 --> 00:05:47.870
Yeah.

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:52.230
And we, in the same way
that we were using Twitter

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.535
in the early days,
everybody was using Twitter,

00:05:54.535 --> 00:05:57.810
Facebook, the only point
of reference when those new

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:02.420
social media platforms came
out at the time was MySpace,

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:05.030
and the whole point of MySpace
was to have as many friends

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.500
as you could possibly have,
you were ranked by how many

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.310
friends you had, so we were like,

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:13.480
of course, I'm on Twitter,
that's why you followed

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:14.820
everybody back on Twitter.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:17.740
In fact, we both had this
thing installed on Twitter

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.670
where we would
automatically send a message

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:21.370
to someone who followed
us and automatically

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:22.540
followed them back.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:26.860
Which has rendered that
aspect of my Twitter useless.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:31.750
And by the way, we're
hearing this very deep

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:32.857
vibration sound.

00:06:32.857 --> 00:06:34.120
Oh good, you're hearing it too.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:36.130
Somebody is doing some
work, it's like somewhere

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:36.963
in the neighborhood.

00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:37.820
Construction?

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:39.265
I don't know if you're
gonna be able to hear it

00:06:39.265 --> 00:06:43.210
in the final mix, but if
you do hear an occasional

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:48.210
vroom, that's what that is,
it's not one of our stomachs,

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:51.170
but we're gonna do our
best to get rid of it.

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:53.500
So I went back a couple of years ago

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.910
and I unfollowed a whole
lot of people on Twitter

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.860
because of that whole point,

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.490
following four or 5,000 people on Twitter

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:01.324
is pointless,

00:07:01.324 --> 00:07:04.030
so I was like, I'll only
follow people that I actually

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:06.340
not just want to be nice to,

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.920
I want to hear what they have to say.

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:11.660
And so, where am I going with this?

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:14.644
So what that means is
that Facebook for me,

00:07:14.644 --> 00:07:18.480
I think you could have
5,000 friends as a limit,

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:20.380
we both got to that place,

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.003
and then I just maintained
this Facebook account

00:07:23.003 --> 00:07:25.470
and when I go on there,

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:28.433
there's a couple of people
that I was friends with,

00:07:28.433 --> 00:07:30.380
that I wasn't really
friends with in real life,

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.180
I think they were in North Carolina,

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:38.640
that throughout the past four
years of the Trump presidency,

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:43.780
seeing what they say about the situation--

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.060
These are friends of friends,

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:46.250
these are not fans.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.790
Correct, correct, these
are people that I was

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.694
connected to in real life in some way.

00:07:51.694 --> 00:07:56.694
Seeing the way they processed
the Trump presidency

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:58.680
was just a fascinating sort of like,

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.650
I felt like it was my personal tie

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:04.630
to a very different perspective

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:06.360
than the perspective that
I had about everything

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:07.347
that happened over the past four years.

00:08:07.347 --> 00:08:10.060
You can dip your toe in,
learn a little something

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:10.997
and then take your toe out.

00:08:10.997 --> 00:08:13.270
Yeah, but I'm not a Facebook debater,

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:15.800
I'm not gonna get in there in
the comments and that whole

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:18.270
thing, but I'd go and
see the people debating.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:19.360
Lurk, you lurk.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:21.780
And so that's why I would go back.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:26.780
Well, one of the things I've
noticed especially this year

00:08:27.147 --> 00:08:30.310
ever since Facebook
has now done the thing,

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:31.870
they've essentially, if
you're looking at Facebook

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:35.808
on your phone, they've
adopted the same practice

00:08:35.808 --> 00:08:40.808
that Instagram has, or even
TikTok where if you click

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:47.050
on the video button on Facebook,
it brings up a full screen

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:54.390
vertical video that plays and
then the next one just comes

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:58.300
up and plays or you can swipe
and the next one comes up

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:00.520
and plays so basically
the same interaction

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.010
that you have with those kinds of things.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:03.773
So you kind of get into this loop.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.070
And this is not a new
phenomenon, but it's something

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:11.410
that I sort of rediscovered,
and I was like,

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:12.920
I think we should talk
about this on Ear Biscuits

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:15.150
because it brought up some
interesting things for us

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:20.150
and that is, the
phenomenon of prank videos

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:25.780
on Facebook combined with
the requirement for videos

00:09:26.791 --> 00:09:31.180
to be at least three minutes
in order to get monetized.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:32.013
Okay.

00:09:32.013 --> 00:09:33.540
Now, there are--

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:34.607
Has rendered what?

00:09:34.607 --> 00:09:36.190
A crazy phenomenon.

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:38.810
It has created this
interesting intersection

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:42.280
of people who are both
making a prank video

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:46.754
but also, okay, trying
to meet the three-minute

00:09:46.754 --> 00:09:49.830
time limit, trying to
get to that three-minute

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:52.530
time limit so they can monetize it,

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:56.120
but they're also, like most prank videos,

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:59.590
99% of prank videos on
the internet, are fake.

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:03.200
So if you take a prank video that is fake,

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.840
and then you require
these people who are not

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.960
professional actors and in
my estimation don't have

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:11.730
a lot of insight into
human behavior in general,

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.690
like what normal rational
human behavior would be.

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:16.990
There's a self-exposing phenomenon.

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:19.380
Having to meet that time limit

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:23.160
exposes the nature of
the thinking and acting

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:26.743
and the execution in a way
that just is fascinating.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:29.230
It lays it bare.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:31.520
And I have a case study video.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:33.358
I have a case study video
that I kind of want to lead

00:10:33.358 --> 00:10:35.900
you through and a couple of
other ones that I can just

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:37.430
talk to you about, but
I want to show you one.

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:40.160
I find it interesting
that you went on Facebook

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.826
to do some political lurking,

00:10:41.826 --> 00:10:46.826
but then a prank video pops
up and then you just found

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.600
yourself watching it, is what happened.

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:55.313
I'm not proud of this,
but my guess would be

00:10:55.313 --> 00:10:57.563
that this never happens to you.

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:02.743
But I would get caught in just sort of,

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:08.310
I'll just get caught in a video
watching sort of just binge

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.080
where there's nothing satisfying at all,

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:14.030
I feel like it's the equivalent
of being at a slot machine.

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:16.640
You're like a toddler
in front of the screen,

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:18.540
that's like, oh there's colors moving,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.990
my eyes are looking at it.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:23.370
I think this happens most often--

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:24.930
It is designed this way.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:27.630
Of course, yeah, I would
think this applies to,

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.170
this is why they made
the interface this way,

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:30.020
this applies to most people,

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:32.520
but TikTok is the perfect example of this,

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:35.430
it's just like, you just kind
of go into this vegetative

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.432
state and just watch a bunch of videos.

00:11:37.432 --> 00:11:38.265
Scroll, scroll.

00:11:38.265 --> 00:11:40.120
But not every other
platform is trying to do

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:40.953
the exact same thing.

00:11:40.953 --> 00:11:42.480
Yeah.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:43.814
And I'd fall victim to it,

00:11:43.814 --> 00:11:46.810
and I dislike myself when that happens,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.970
but at least I discovered
something that I thought

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:50.130
would make a good Ear Biscuits,

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:52.308
so at least we're monetizing it.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:56.910
So you watched this
video and you're gonna,

