So one of the things that
keeps people in a good story,

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:12.280
one of the reasons that
you like the movies

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:14.620
and the TV shows that you like,

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:19.250
is because the way people react
to unbelievable situations

00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:21.810
is incredibly believable and relatable.

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:25.060
It might not be that
it makes a lot of sense

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:27.960
that people might end up on an island,

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:30.470
like they did in Lost
and not be able to tell,

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:32.420
there's unrealistic things that happened,

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:34.760
but people's reactions to the situation

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:36.350
are believable and relatable,

00:24:36.350 --> 00:24:39.437
and that's what doesn't
happen in these videos.

00:24:39.437 --> 00:24:42.370
A third video, super simple,

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:44.630
there's a woman and again these videos

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:46.780
came up, they were served to me,

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:50.180
Mr. I'm Watching Fake
Prank Videos on Facebook,

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:52.430
with a vengeance, they
were served up to me

00:24:52.430 --> 00:24:53.930
because Facebook apparently understood,

00:24:53.930 --> 00:24:56.086
he likes these fake videos.

00:24:56.086 --> 00:24:58.803
Because you watched all of the first one.

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:02.613
The retention time was full
because I was studying it.

00:25:02.613 --> 00:25:03.480
And you watched the second one.

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:07.200
And then the third one
comes up and there's a woman

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:10.200
standing knee deep in
a lake next to a dock

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:11.940
and there is a man behind her

00:25:11.940 --> 00:25:16.350
and he has an aligator
head on a fishing line,

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:17.510
and he puts it in the water,

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:19.880
and he's like, hey, how are
the reservations coming,

00:25:19.880 --> 00:25:22.570
like she's making
reservations on the phone.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:23.620
While standing in water?

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:26.150
Standing in water, facing away from him.

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:27.510
And of course I don't
know who the person is

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:29.670
who's filming this interaction
and why she's not paying

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:32.910
attention to them, let's
not think about that.

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:35.310
And she says, oh yeah,
it's taking a second.

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:39.380
And then he's like, well,
you need to be watchful

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:41.310
when you're in the water
because there's all kinds

00:25:41.310 --> 00:25:44.530
of stuff in lakes like, sea snakes.

00:25:44.530 --> 00:25:46.710
He literally said that, alligators.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:47.880
And she's like, "Alligators?"

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:49.700
Alligators are my number one fear

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:50.890
and you know that.

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:52.840
But she's not turning around to see it.

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:57.250
So he has to maintain, again
to get to the three minute

00:25:57.250 --> 00:26:01.540
limit, which I didn't
talk about, the way they

00:26:01.540 --> 00:26:03.030
stall that, the mechanic video

00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:04.910
is she kept handing him tools--

00:26:04.910 --> 00:26:05.743
Oh my gosh.

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:10.350
They had to pick the
moment in which they stall

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:12.590
the video to keep you watching
to get to that three minute

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:14.940
mark and in the gator
video, it's literally

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:18.280
just the husband moving
the gator around behind her

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:19.900
and continuing the conversation like,

00:26:19.900 --> 00:26:22.280
how are the reservations coming,

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:23.320
for three minutes.

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:24.153
And then eventually--

00:26:24.153 --> 00:26:25.180
Do you still not like gators?

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:28.040
And she sees the gator
and freaks out, whatever.

00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:32.290
So I believe we said this,
but it bears saying it again,

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:36.250
you don't get paid by
Facebook for your videos

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:38.794
unless they break the three minute mark.

00:26:38.794 --> 00:26:42.403
It's created a prank monster.

00:26:44.481 --> 00:26:46.630
And you'd think it would expose pranks.

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:47.633
That's the point.

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:50.500
I think that's the underlying point,

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:53.944
is that Facebook doesn't care about this

00:26:53.944 --> 00:26:58.366
because they make money and
apparently the people making

00:26:58.366 --> 00:26:59.870
the videos don't care.

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:03.920
It's not like they take
it as a personal insult

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:05.790
that you find it fake, they just go

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:06.911
and make their next one.

00:27:06.911 --> 00:27:10.300
They have embraced, my
theory is that people who are

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:12.860
making these videos
have embraced the fact,

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:15.533
of course it's fake, I'm doing my best

00:27:15.533 --> 00:27:17.060
to make you believe that it's fake,

00:27:17.060 --> 00:27:20.010
or maybe there's a certain
small sliver of the population

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:23.772
that's not that smart, that
does believe that this is real,

00:27:23.772 --> 00:27:27.070
but we're all agreeing
with the fact that yeah,

00:27:27.070 --> 00:27:29.770
this is fake, it's not that big of a deal,

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:31.620
but you watched it and I
made money so I'm gonna make

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:32.570
the next one.

00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:36.290
And if people dislike the video enough,

00:27:36.290 --> 00:27:39.270
these algorithms do
take that into account,

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:42.837
but you know, the main thing
is if people are gonna watch

00:27:42.837 --> 00:27:45.799
the whole video, there's
no denying the fact that

00:27:45.799 --> 00:27:49.330
it kept people on the platform

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:51.810
and it takes a lot to override that

00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:56.650
and they're not gonna program
the algorithm to outweigh

00:27:57.660 --> 00:28:01.440
likes to dislikes or ratio
or whoever they look at that

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:03.463
to whether you watched it or not.

00:28:04.670 --> 00:28:09.670
The principle with Netflix,
it's a different thing,

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:13.350
but it's a similar principle, that Netflix

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:15.980
just started showing
you recommended things

00:28:15.980 --> 00:28:18.240
that you'd think you'd like to watch

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:20.580
and leaning on that instead of the queue.

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:22.430
When Netflix first came around,

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:24.460
it was all about the queue,

00:28:24.460 --> 00:28:26.770
it was all about I want
to watch this next,

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:30.030
this next, this next and you
would just go through it,

00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:32.890
but the discovery mechanisms were,

00:28:32.890 --> 00:28:36.810
they had a long way to
develop and they since have,

00:28:36.810 --> 00:28:40.930
so now Netflix is mostly about that,

00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:42.771
as are all of the streaming services.

00:28:42.771 --> 00:28:46.120
And by the way that's
why YouTube subscriptions

00:28:46.120 --> 00:28:48.313
have been buried for years.

00:28:49.190 --> 00:28:51.230
For this principle that like,

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:54.280
it's not what's your stated intention,

00:28:54.280 --> 00:28:55.610
it's your--

00:28:55.610 --> 00:28:56.443
Behavior.

00:28:56.443 --> 00:28:59.378
Actual actions that
are the tell-tale signs

00:28:59.378 --> 00:29:02.850
of what's gonna work and
then what we can sell

00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:03.840
the most against.

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:07.280
And this is actually, this illustrates,

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:09.003
I'm gonna make a political,

00:29:10.030 --> 00:29:12.133
I guess you could call it a
political point at this point,

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:18.950
because this is an
example of why when profit

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:23.930
is the primary motive behind a process,

00:29:23.930 --> 00:29:27.810
it doesn't, I mean, I
grew up a conservative guy

00:29:27.810 --> 00:29:31.080
who thought that this
idea of the free market

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:34.170
and the fact that everybody
being motivated by money

00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:37.030
will ultimately bring out
the best possible outcome,

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:39.190
right, like a pure capitalistic view.

00:29:39.190 --> 00:29:40.080
I've since--

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:41.870
Competition makes everything better.

00:29:41.870 --> 00:29:44.210
I've since re-calibrated that view.

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:47.950
I'm not well-versed on these
issues, but there's so many

00:29:47.950 --> 00:29:52.310
examples in modern society
of how profit being the goal

00:29:52.310 --> 00:29:55.533
causes things to, first of
all, it causes a lot of people

00:29:55.533 --> 00:29:57.840
to suffer, I'm not saying
anybody is suffering

00:29:57.840 --> 00:29:59.233
in this situation necessarily,

00:29:59.233 --> 00:30:02.750
but you know, speaking of
Facebook and all social media

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:06.300
in general, we've seen
that this whole idea of

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:08.966
keeping people on the platform
and keeping people watching

00:30:08.966 --> 00:30:12.751
ads has led to this incredible
political polarization

00:30:12.751 --> 00:30:15.590
in our country, as was very well explored,

00:30:15.590 --> 00:30:18.638
in the Social Dilemma
documentary on Netflix.

00:30:18.638 --> 00:30:22.810
But this is just an example
of profit being a motivation

00:30:22.810 --> 00:30:25.820
leading to a very bad product.

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:27.900
This hasn't led to a great product.

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:28.900
Have you no pride?

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:31.790
This has led to something that truly sucks

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:35.260
in every sense of the
word, fake prank videos

00:30:35.260 --> 00:30:37.657
that are, fake prank videos suck.

00:30:37.657 --> 00:30:38.710
Period.

00:30:38.710 --> 00:30:39.543
Yeah.

00:30:39.543 --> 00:30:42.880
Fake prank videos that are
drawn out for three minutes

00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:44.250
in order to make a profit for the person

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:46.290
who has decided to make
a living in this way--

00:30:46.290 --> 00:30:47.123
Are awesome.

00:30:47.123 --> 00:30:48.590
Suck squared!

00:30:48.590 --> 00:30:50.640
Which makes it kind of awesome.

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:51.473
And so--

00:30:51.473 --> 00:30:52.495
I might get back on Facebook.

00:30:52.495 --> 00:30:55.674
I'm just making the point
that that isn't the case.

00:30:55.674 --> 00:31:00.100
I think that what makes
the human collective better

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:03.032
and is beneficial to all
of us is a better motivator

00:31:03.032 --> 00:31:07.400
than profit, but again off the soapbox.

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:11.327
As a sidenote, arguably, our entire career

00:31:11.327 --> 00:31:15.070
and success, post Good Mythical Morning,

00:31:15.070 --> 00:31:17.850
or during, we're still
doing Good Mythical Morning,

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:20.200
but you know, post the launch
of Good Mythical Morning

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:23.033
is based on this algorithmic phenomenon

00:31:23.033 --> 00:31:24.880
of watch time as well, you know?

00:31:24.880 --> 00:31:25.713
Yeah.

00:31:25.713 --> 00:31:26.546
We benefit from this.

00:31:26.546 --> 00:31:28.610
But we didn't know that.

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:30.310
We wanted to have a conversation,

00:31:30.310 --> 00:31:34.549
we formatted a show, we made
something that we were proud

00:31:34.549 --> 00:31:38.870
of and our team is committed to excellence

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:41.640
and doing the best we
can and so very quickly

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:46.013
we arrived at, to have a
meaningful conversation,

00:31:46.013 --> 00:31:49.330
you were at the 12 minute
mark, but we were actually

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:52.420
cautioned to not go far
beyond the 10 minute mark,

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:57.420
but then YouTube changed from
emphasizing subscriptions

00:31:57.500 --> 00:32:00.090
to watch time and rewarding that.

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:05.090
So we made the best product
which started getting,

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:06.220
it broke 10 minutes,

00:32:06.220 --> 00:32:10.540
got closer to 15 minutes
and people stuck around

00:32:10.540 --> 00:32:11.830
and they watched the whole thing

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:16.600
and so we were rewarded,
because we rewarded the viewer

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:18.670
and they just kept binge watching,

00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:20.120
we had so many of the episodes

00:32:20.120 --> 00:32:24.030
that everybody won in that situation.

00:32:24.030 --> 00:32:27.640
That's an example of the
content being motivated

00:32:27.640 --> 00:32:31.470
by the system and the profit,

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:36.133
but making a product that
fits within that system,

00:32:37.250 --> 00:32:39.670
that hasn't compromised
itself for the sake

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:41.666
of the profit.

00:32:41.666 --> 00:32:44.510
We didn't know about the
principle when we were formatting

00:32:44.510 --> 00:32:45.610
Good Mythical Morning.

00:32:45.610 --> 00:32:50.610
But now, as we're reformatting
Good Mythical Morning,

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:54.039
and creating cuts for Facebook,

00:32:54.039 --> 00:32:59.039
or making new sketches
that we put on Instagram,

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:02.333
we absolutely have to take into account

00:33:02.333 --> 00:33:05.960
what's the threshold
to be able to monetize.

00:33:05.960 --> 00:33:07.910
You know, we can't just make videos

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:12.040
and not be able to and
then platform refuses

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:13.210
to run ads against them.

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:16.130
So, Facebook, you know,
that three minute mark

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:17.770
means a lot to us too.

00:33:17.770 --> 00:33:20.720
So if you're watching on
Facebook, and by the way,

00:33:20.720 --> 00:33:22.930
that's how my mom watches
Good Mythical Mornings,

00:33:22.930 --> 00:33:25.271
every I talk to her,
another thing she'll say is,

00:33:25.271 --> 00:33:28.780
I've been watching more
of your show lately,

00:33:28.780 --> 00:33:30.920
and she'll talk about a
video and I'll be like,

00:33:30.920 --> 00:33:34.009
that video is like four years old, mom.

00:33:34.009 --> 00:33:36.090
I was like, you need to
look at our hair styles

00:33:36.090 --> 00:33:37.040
to really figure it out,

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:40.349
because the way that
Facebook will server it up,

00:33:40.349 --> 00:33:43.003
it's seemingly random.

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:45.890
Once you watch an old one,

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:48.200
and sometimes we repost stuff.

00:33:48.200 --> 00:33:49.070
Right.

00:33:49.070 --> 00:33:51.400
But the main thing we're doing is we make

00:33:52.920 --> 00:33:56.440
cuts of Good Mythical
Morning, cut-downs we call it,

00:33:56.440 --> 00:33:58.450
that break the three
minute mark, but that work

00:33:58.450 --> 00:33:59.920
within the Facebook environment.

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:01.667
But again, they break
the three minute mark,

00:34:01.667 --> 00:34:04.580
but because they're 12,
15-minute episodes--

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:05.430
And that's easy.

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:07.123
It's an easy thing to cut it down,

00:34:07.123 --> 00:34:08.600
it's something that we're proud of.

00:34:08.600 --> 00:34:10.260
The place that we kind of relate to this

00:34:10.260 --> 00:34:11.710
a little bit is Instagram.

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:14.046
You know, IGTV, there's monetization there

00:34:14.046 --> 00:34:18.993
as well, it's in its infancy,
and it's not that significant.

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:23.950
But, the threshold for
monetization on Instagram

00:34:23.950 --> 00:34:28.950
is two minutes, so the way
that we try to address that

00:34:30.530 --> 00:34:32.840
is not by taking a concept that,

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:34.450
I think the thing that all these videos,

00:34:34.450 --> 00:34:36.350
these prank videos that
I just talked about,

00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:40.591
especially the alligator
one and the bike thief one.

00:34:40.591 --> 00:34:44.530
You could tell this story in
a much more compelling way

00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:47.033
in about 30 seconds, right?

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:54.010
Now, we create sketches that
from the conceptualization

00:34:54.290 --> 00:34:56.623
and the writing and the
execution of the sketches,

00:34:56.623 --> 00:34:59.143
it's like, okay, is this
a concept that makes sense

00:34:59.143 --> 00:35:00.430
in two minutes?

00:35:00.430 --> 00:35:02.023
We don't stretch anything out.

00:35:03.230 --> 00:35:07.830
So yes, we are playing this
game, and this is a business

00:35:07.830 --> 00:35:10.670
and so we're not going to take the time

00:35:10.670 --> 00:35:14.930
to dress up and be characters
and make this video

00:35:14.930 --> 00:35:17.640
just for kicks, it's part of,

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:19.010
if we can make this
thing two minutes long,

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:21.010
we can actually get paid to do it, okay.

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:22.350
So let's make it two minutes long.

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:24.635
But we have artistic pride.

00:35:29.480 --> 00:35:30.427
That is questionable sometimes but--

00:35:30.427 --> 00:35:32.787
No, we do, we do, we are,

00:35:32.787 --> 00:35:35.660
this is something that we've always been,

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:40.660
I've always felt dirty about the system

00:35:40.970 --> 00:35:43.350
and even, I think the best example of this

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:46.550
is the titling and
thumbnailing of YouTube videos,

00:35:46.550 --> 00:35:48.290
which is a whole conversation
that we can have,

00:35:48.290 --> 00:35:50.340
but just to quickly cover it,

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:53.690
you know the reason that
our thumbnails on YouTube

00:35:53.690 --> 00:35:55.770
look the way they do--

00:35:55.770 --> 00:35:56.603
Obnoxious.

00:35:56.603 --> 00:35:59.600
Is not because we think
that they look good,

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:01.300
I mean just to be frank with you,

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:06.300
I do not like to see me making faces

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:10.210
that close up, I don't, I don't like it.

00:36:10.210 --> 00:36:12.670
I hate it.

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:14.540
I'm pretty close to you right now,

00:36:14.540 --> 00:36:19.020
this is like, I mean, I
don't even have to extend

00:36:19.020 --> 00:36:20.920
my arm all the way to poke your eye out,

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:22.750
that's how close I am sitting to you,

00:36:22.750 --> 00:36:25.700
and this is the
approximation of how close,

00:36:25.700 --> 00:36:28.420
if you were recreating
the thumbnail experience

00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:29.260
in real life.

00:36:29.260 --> 00:36:30.690
Yeah, right.

00:36:30.690 --> 00:36:32.860
I think psychologically,

00:36:32.860 --> 00:36:34.813
look at me, look at me and do it,

00:36:34.813 --> 00:36:38.070
if you're this close to me,
making faces like that--

00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:38.903
You want to slap me.

00:36:38.903 --> 00:36:40.200
And you were about to eat something.

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:42.000
You want to slap me.

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:45.170
Why would you make eye contact with me

00:36:45.170 --> 00:36:47.810
while eating at this close of a range,

00:36:47.810 --> 00:36:50.288
much less when making
that face you're making?

00:36:50.288 --> 00:36:53.323
I want to, I want to slap me too.

00:36:53.323 --> 00:36:54.370
That's not human, man.

00:36:54.370 --> 00:36:56.380
Every time I see one
of our GMM thumbnails,

00:36:56.380 --> 00:36:58.823
I want to slap me or
you, whoever is featured.

00:36:59.720 --> 00:37:01.598
And I'm assuming lots of
people feel the same way,

00:37:01.598 --> 00:37:02.970
but you know what?

00:37:03.943 --> 00:37:05.410
They got a thumbs up, thumbs down,

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:06.510
they should add a slap.

00:37:06.510 --> 00:37:09.970
Through doing this for a
decade and fine-tuning that.

00:37:09.970 --> 00:37:11.120
We're slaves to that.

00:37:11.120 --> 00:37:14.630
What we have determined is
that that style of thumbnail,

00:37:14.630 --> 00:37:16.300
and there's a lot of different factors

00:37:16.300 --> 00:37:19.360
that go into that style
of thumbnail working

00:37:19.360 --> 00:37:23.030
is what gets us the most views.

00:37:23.030 --> 00:37:26.100
So like you said, we
got artistic integrity,

00:37:26.100 --> 00:37:27.820
but we are, we do play the game,

00:37:27.820 --> 00:37:29.580
we are willing to compromise,

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:32.650
we do A-B testing on both
titles and thumbnails

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:34.650
every single morning,
it's like, hey this one

00:37:34.650 --> 00:37:36.990
is not really catching,
we think we need to change

00:37:36.990 --> 00:37:39.070
this word to this, that's why
if you watch from the very

00:37:39.070 --> 00:37:41.100
beginning every day,
you'll see some changes

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:42.150
because hey, this is a game,

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:42.983
we're trying to play, we're trying to win

00:37:42.983 --> 00:37:44.670
and we're playing within the system.

00:37:44.670 --> 00:37:46.935
We personally are not involved in that.

00:37:46.935 --> 00:37:47.768
Thank God.

00:37:47.768 --> 00:37:50.290
Because we just cannot,
even talking about it

00:37:50.290 --> 00:37:51.630
is demoralizing.

00:37:51.630 --> 00:37:54.400
But the one thing that we couldn't do

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:56.022
is make that prank video,

00:37:56.022 --> 00:38:01.022
I just couldn't, so we checked
some pride at the door.

00:38:02.600 --> 00:38:04.240
We try to maintain some integrity though,

00:38:04.240 --> 00:38:06.904
because while I do want
to slap you when I see you

00:38:06.904 --> 00:38:09.704
on the thumbnail, and also
myself, so it goes both ways,

00:38:10.670 --> 00:38:12.800
what I'm doing in the
thumbnail, what you're doing

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:14.610
in the thumbnail is not misleading,

00:38:14.610 --> 00:38:17.220
it is indicative of what
they're gonna see in the video

00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:19.490
and the title is also indicative.

00:38:19.490 --> 00:38:22.200
They may be sometimes
just a little sensational,

00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:24.561
maybe a little bit of a misleading thing,

00:38:24.561 --> 00:38:29.560
but as a principle, it is
supposed to be indicative,

00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:31.600
because we understand that
there's a long term relationship

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:33.160
that we have with our
audience, and if we begin

00:38:33.160 --> 00:38:35.280
to mislead the audience and take you

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:37.152
as a bunch of fools, you're
not gonna stick around

00:38:37.152 --> 00:38:38.070
for very long.

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:39.883
Right and that's the other
thing, that's insulting.

00:38:39.883 --> 00:38:43.763
So we have a history with prank videos.

00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:47.563
As creators, as artists.

00:38:48.780 --> 00:38:52.498
And we couldn't bear to keep it up.

00:38:52.498 --> 00:38:57.160
I will say, one of our first videos is

00:38:59.920 --> 00:39:03.030
trimming strangers' nose hairs at the mall

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:05.500
and this was made before YouTube existed,

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:07.150
'cause we were making videos

00:39:08.079 --> 00:39:12.060
when we were involved in
crew and we were hosting

00:39:12.980 --> 00:39:16.330
or MCing the regional
Christmas conference,

00:39:16.330 --> 00:39:18.600
we made comedy videos,
we talked about this

00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:21.490
in the last year's and that's
where this video came from,

00:39:21.490 --> 00:39:25.750
from the, I think it
was the Greensboro mall.

00:39:28.003 --> 00:39:30.570
The Christmas conference
was in Greensboro,

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:33.807
and you know, it was just,
hey we need to make a video,

00:39:33.807 --> 00:39:38.020
we made sketches, we were writing songs,

00:39:38.020 --> 00:39:39.270
but we would also do prank videos,

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:41.550
it was just a genre that you did.

00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:42.760
But we really did it,

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:46.250
we really took a nose trimmer to the mall

00:39:46.250 --> 00:39:49.830
and wore these, you can look it up

00:39:49.830 --> 00:39:52.380
on the Rhett and Link
YouTube channel and watch it,

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:55.990
I think, I'm pretty
sure it's still public,

00:39:55.990 --> 00:39:57.830
it shouldn't be, it's horrible.

00:39:57.830 --> 00:39:59.401
But the thing that's not horrible,

00:39:59.401 --> 00:40:03.170
the redemptive value is that you can tell

00:40:03.170 --> 00:40:05.401
that we really went up
to strangers and trimmed

00:40:05.401 --> 00:40:06.720
their nose hairs.

00:40:06.720 --> 00:40:09.985
People would, you could
talk people into doing that.

00:40:09.985 --> 00:40:14.985
And it's implied that we're
just going around the mall

00:40:15.380 --> 00:40:18.300
doing this, so I'm about
to trim your nose hairs

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:21.110
and I've just trimmed who
knows how many other people's

00:40:21.110 --> 00:40:21.943
nose hairs.

00:40:21.943 --> 00:40:22.776
With the same device.

00:40:22.776 --> 00:40:23.609
It was heinous.

00:40:23.609 --> 00:40:25.850
Yeah, but it never crossed
our minds that we would

00:40:25.850 --> 00:40:26.850
fake it.

00:40:26.850 --> 00:40:29.380
That's the beauty of it, the beauty of it

00:40:29.380 --> 00:40:32.112
was getting the real reaction from people.

00:40:32.112 --> 00:40:32.945
That's what we were into.

00:40:32.945 --> 00:40:34.563
The fact that we really did it,

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:39.500
I mean, for me, whenever
we come up with something

00:40:39.500 --> 00:40:41.240
like that, I'm like, this is go time,

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:44.370
we've got to show that we
got the balls to do this.

00:40:44.370 --> 00:40:46.997
And to your credit, you
were right there with me,

00:40:46.997 --> 00:40:50.048
but there was a lot more
inner turmoil with you

00:40:50.048 --> 00:40:51.270
than there was with me.

00:40:51.270 --> 00:40:53.910
I could just switch off my conscience.

00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:55.154
Yeah, I don't like messing with people.

00:40:55.154 --> 00:40:57.705
I have to make this work.

00:40:57.705 --> 00:40:59.550
I would say that--

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:03.180
Because we've got to make a funny video,

00:41:03.180 --> 00:41:04.360
we've got to do this.

00:41:04.360 --> 00:41:06.830
It's not just that, it's not that you have

00:41:06.830 --> 00:41:08.690
a stronger commitment
to making a funny video,

00:41:08.690 --> 00:41:13.163
I think it's that you are
much more comfortable,

00:41:13.163 --> 00:41:15.900
in life in general, in
making people uncomfortable.

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:19.820
And I don't know what this says about me,

00:41:19.820 --> 00:41:23.480
but I have a real difficult
time making people

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:24.630
feel uncomfortable,

00:41:24.630 --> 00:41:26.940
that's why I do the sympathetic laughing,

00:41:26.940 --> 00:41:29.530
you know, like if you
watch, when we have guests,

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:31.390
and people point this,
like when we have guests,

00:41:31.390 --> 00:41:33.220
when we used to have guests on GMM,

00:41:33.220 --> 00:41:35.871
before COVID, we will have them again,

00:41:35.871 --> 00:41:37.920
I'll laugh at everything they say

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:41.047
because there's this, and
I think it's just called

00:41:41.047 --> 00:41:43.720
sympathetic laughing and it's basically,

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:48.460
you laugh to subtly communicate
that you're not a threat,

00:41:48.460 --> 00:41:50.660
and try to make the
person feel comfortable.

00:41:51.855 --> 00:41:53.470
I find myself doing that often.

00:41:53.470 --> 00:41:56.440
I find it interesting
that in this conversation,

00:41:56.440 --> 00:41:58.470
you're the one who's a
little more on the defensive

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:03.470
than me, but I'm basically the
one who is in these videos,

00:42:03.830 --> 00:42:06.853
much more the willing jackass.

00:42:08.200 --> 00:42:10.140
I'll watch that stuff back
and I don't want to be that

00:42:10.140 --> 00:42:13.860
person anymore, it starts
to eat away at who you are,

00:42:13.860 --> 00:42:17.143
you know, because I guess
I'll kind of switch.

00:42:18.330 --> 00:42:20.830
But for the sake of these
kinds of videos, it's better

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:21.970
to be that way though.

00:42:21.970 --> 00:42:23.180
Sure.

00:42:23.180 --> 00:42:25.400
But my point is, that
was one of the factors

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:28.130
that took us away from doing prank videos.

00:42:28.130 --> 00:42:33.130
The other one was, yeah,
because it starts to make

00:42:34.110 --> 00:42:36.090
both of us feel icky inside,

00:42:36.090 --> 00:42:37.430
to take advantage of people.

00:42:37.430 --> 00:42:41.790
I think, my theory is that
it was a cultural change

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:45.520
that took place, not that prank videos

00:42:45.520 --> 00:42:50.060
are not still a thing, but
there's a generational change,

00:42:50.060 --> 00:42:52.410
and especially when all the other content

00:42:52.410 --> 00:42:54.330
that you make is generally--

00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:55.163
Nice.

00:42:55.163 --> 00:42:56.020
Nice and good-hearted.

00:42:56.020 --> 00:42:59.280
Like, you know, we try to uplift people,

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:02.810
our comedy is not cynical,
observations we try to make

00:43:02.810 --> 00:43:05.590
about people are not
demoralizing or derogatory

00:43:05.590 --> 00:43:09.927
in general, so then, but
we still have this really,

00:43:09.927 --> 00:43:13.270
like, we're fascinated with
people and we do both enjoy

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:16.350
putting people into
uncomfortable situations,

00:43:16.350 --> 00:43:17.790
and that's what our local commercials were

00:43:17.790 --> 00:43:19.490
in a lot of ways.

00:43:19.490 --> 00:43:20.950
We were walking this line.

00:43:20.950 --> 00:43:23.780
Yeah, but screwing with
people at the dirt track race,

00:43:23.780 --> 00:43:25.313
with the fake survey, remember that video?

00:43:25.313 --> 00:43:26.970
That a lot harder for me.

00:43:26.970 --> 00:43:28.240
It's much easier for me--

00:43:28.240 --> 00:43:29.600
You're lying to people.

00:43:29.600 --> 00:43:31.398
Right.
So you can laugh at them.

00:43:31.398 --> 00:43:34.412
But it's a much finer line
with the local commercials

00:43:34.412 --> 00:43:36.870
because we're doing something that's like,

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:38.220
this is gonna benefit your business,

00:43:38.220 --> 00:43:40.320
and we're also collaborating,

00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:42.420
this is an idea that
you either came up with

00:43:42.420 --> 00:43:44.600
or you bought into, but
we're gonna stand here

00:43:44.600 --> 00:43:47.320
and get you to say this line
knowing that it's gonna be

00:43:47.320 --> 00:43:49.730
funny for reasons that you
may not fully understand,

00:43:49.730 --> 00:43:50.860
I'm much more comfortable with that,

00:43:50.860 --> 00:43:53.440
but when I have to be literally
interacting with somebody

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:55.323
on camera, it's just
harder for me to do it.

00:43:55.323 --> 00:43:57.720
There's a cultural shift,
but at the same time,

00:43:57.720 --> 00:43:58.553
there's not.

00:43:58.553 --> 00:44:00.090
These videos are still going,

00:44:00.090 --> 00:44:02.240
like tens of millions
of views on Facebook.

00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:03.373
They're all fake.

00:44:04.260 --> 00:44:06.190
They're not all fake.

00:44:06.190 --> 00:44:08.590
And you look at the success
of the Impractical Jokers,

00:44:08.590 --> 00:44:10.333
now that's an older audience.

00:44:11.330 --> 00:44:12.230
I think it's generational.

00:44:12.230 --> 00:44:14.290
The savvy feeling of feeling sorry

00:44:14.290 --> 00:44:15.418
for the younger audience.

00:44:15.418 --> 00:44:18.170
My kids loved Impractical Jokers.

00:44:18.170 --> 00:44:19.904
And I think, correct me if I'm wrong,

00:44:19.904 --> 00:44:24.570
I've never watched it, but
I think they make themselves

00:44:24.570 --> 00:44:26.763
the butt of the joke a lot of the time.

00:44:27.610 --> 00:44:30.518
They're primarily trying
to embarrass one another

00:44:30.518 --> 00:44:31.687
as friends.

00:44:31.687 --> 00:44:33.170
And they--

00:44:33.170 --> 00:44:34.750
It's much more about that than it is about

00:44:34.750 --> 00:44:36.170
making people feel bed.
They never leave the lie,

00:44:36.170 --> 00:44:38.550
but even if they make somebody feel bad,

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:41.433
they let them in on the
joke at the end, I think.

00:44:42.440 --> 00:44:43.760
They may do that in real life.

00:44:43.760 --> 00:44:45.960
That is the principle of the big--

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:46.860
But they don't do that.

00:44:46.860 --> 00:44:49.050
It's not, candid camera always ended,

00:44:49.050 --> 00:44:51.340
like the old candid camera
show always ended with,

00:44:51.340 --> 00:44:53.840
hey man, I'm just joking,
there's a hidden camera,

00:44:53.840 --> 00:44:55.480
there's a hidden camera,
they don't do that

00:44:55.480 --> 00:44:56.340
on Impractical Jokers.

00:44:56.340 --> 00:44:58.340
They may do that off camera,

00:44:58.340 --> 00:44:59.790
but they don't make that part of the bit,

00:44:59.790 --> 00:45:01.180
it's just not a part of it.

00:45:01.180 --> 00:45:04.150
I think the last prank video we made

00:45:05.510 --> 00:45:07.140
just broke our spirit,

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:10.195
and we were like, never
again will we do this.

00:45:10.195 --> 00:45:12.110
And so now if you haven't done it before,

00:45:12.110 --> 00:45:13.040
there's a confession to make.

00:45:13.040 --> 00:45:16.720
I feel like we need to make
the confession up front

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:18.316
because it will make watching the video

00:45:18.316 --> 00:45:22.000
and analysis of it that
much more insightful.

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:25.470
This video is called invisible dog prank,

00:45:25.470 --> 00:45:28.106
released on our Rhett &amp; Link channel,

00:45:28.106 --> 00:45:31.140
it's about to break 2 million views.

00:45:31.140 --> 00:45:35.350
It was released February 26th 2013.

00:45:35.350 --> 00:45:40.253
And there are elements of
this video that were faked.

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:43.960
Now, this video was a sponsored--

00:45:43.960 --> 00:45:45.650
I don't know how sorry I am yet.

00:45:45.650 --> 00:45:46.810
We need to talk it through.

00:45:46.810 --> 00:45:49.170
This video was sponsored,

00:45:49.170 --> 00:45:52.530
and there were these new headphones,

00:45:52.530 --> 00:45:54.866
from, was it--

00:45:54.866 --> 00:45:55.699
DTS.

00:45:55.699 --> 00:45:56.933
DTS.

00:45:57.930 --> 00:46:00.690
Yeah, DTS had these new headphones called

00:46:00.690 --> 00:46:04.630
X-Technology, it was
3-D like surround sound

00:46:04.630 --> 00:46:08.240
and so we were gonna go
to their headquarters

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:10.780
which was out west of here,

00:46:10.780 --> 00:46:15.230
and the ideas was let's make
a video where to demonstrate

00:46:15.230 --> 00:46:18.800
how realistic this 3-D sounds is,

00:46:18.800 --> 00:46:22.460
we're going to make people
think that there is a dog

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:26.110
about to attack them from
behind while they were doing,

00:46:26.110 --> 00:46:27.290
I don't even know what we told them

00:46:27.290 --> 00:46:28.900
we were gonna do, we'll
figure that out in the video.

00:46:28.900 --> 00:46:31.070
Let's watch, we'll watch
the video together.

00:46:31.070 --> 00:46:36.070
But yeah, I remember why
we constructed this concept

00:46:36.130 --> 00:46:40.240
and then sold it to DTS,
and we can talk about that

00:46:40.240 --> 00:46:42.240
afterwards, so let's
just, it's a three-minute

00:46:42.240 --> 00:46:45.822
42 seconds video, so hey,
it could go on Facebook.

00:46:45.822 --> 00:46:48.273
It's 42 seconds longer
than it needs to be.

00:46:50.000 --> 00:46:52.130
This is Bullet, he's
actually a pretty nice dog.

00:46:52.130 --> 00:46:53.689
But he's been trained to do this.

00:46:55.428 --> 00:46:57.010
We're gonna make people
think they're being attacked

00:46:57.010 --> 00:46:58.070
by him.

00:46:58.070 --> 00:47:00.780
But it will just be in their headphones.

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:01.613
Here's the setup.

00:47:01.613 --> 00:47:03.460
DTS has developed a
technology that creates

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:06.120
a realistic, 3-D like
surround sound experience

00:47:06.120 --> 00:47:07.680
through just a pair of headphones.

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:09.190
But our test subjects don't know that,

00:47:09.190 --> 00:47:10.990
they think they're just
coming in for a hearing test

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:11.823
in a focus group.

00:47:11.823 --> 00:47:14.040
This is at their studio, at the DTS.

00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:17.840
So we were playing DTS employees,

00:47:17.840 --> 00:47:19.530
that why we had the shirts on.

00:47:19.530 --> 00:47:20.523
Yeah, like testing new technology.

00:47:20.523 --> 00:47:21.533
When we bring them into our testing area,

00:47:21.533 --> 00:47:24.240
we'll have Bullet bark at them.

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:25.073
Scary dog.

00:47:25.073 --> 00:47:26.570
And in the middle of our lame test,

00:47:26.570 --> 00:47:28.660
we introduce the surround
sound of Bullet attacking them

00:47:28.660 --> 00:47:29.493
from behind.

00:47:30.549 --> 00:47:32.180
But when they turn around,

00:47:32.180 --> 00:47:33.410
there's no dog.

00:47:33.410 --> 00:47:36.820
Just a grumpy-looking cat, named Purrfect.

00:47:36.820 --> 00:47:38.930
So first of all, we also got.

00:47:38.930 --> 00:47:40.670
Is that Grumpy Cat?

00:47:40.670 --> 00:47:43.580
No, it's a Grumpy Cat
lookalike, may he rest in peace.

00:47:43.580 --> 00:47:45.634
Dude, was Grumpy Cat a thing a that point?

00:47:45.634 --> 00:47:46.980
Oh yeah.

00:47:46.980 --> 00:47:49.660
So we didn't come up with Grumpy Cat.

00:47:49.660 --> 00:47:50.990
Don't want to give us that much credit.

00:47:50.990 --> 00:47:51.947
But it's like, you think it's a dog,

00:47:51.947 --> 00:47:53.170
and you turn around and see a cat,

00:47:53.170 --> 00:47:56.540
this is a fun, fun prank.

00:47:56.540 --> 00:47:59.030
However, here's what happened.

00:47:59.030 --> 00:48:02.053
On the day, now they funny thing is.

00:48:02.053 --> 00:48:03.853
Not on the day, in planning.

00:48:04.990 --> 00:48:09.120
In planning, we were like,
this video has to work.

00:48:09.120 --> 00:48:13.980
So we need some insurance
that if we don't get reactions

00:48:13.980 --> 00:48:18.950
that are, you know,
shareable, and buzz-worthy,

00:48:18.950 --> 00:48:21.900
we have to have a plan B to ensure that.

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:25.130
So you know, we were adamant
that we were gonna conduct

00:48:25.130 --> 00:48:27.300
the prank legitimately.

00:48:27.300 --> 00:48:28.768
Now I don't know if this is me moralizing

00:48:28.768 --> 00:48:31.323
in my own head, but that
is not how I remember it.

00:48:32.180 --> 00:48:33.013
What I remember--

00:48:33.013 --> 00:48:35.350
I know that's what we did,
and then what we would do,

00:48:35.350 --> 00:48:37.317
as we watch it, I might be
able to pick out people,

00:48:37.317 --> 00:48:39.113
but I think what we did was,

00:48:39.950 --> 00:48:41.580
we would get people to--

00:48:41.580 --> 00:48:42.799
We just got people to do it twice.

00:48:42.799 --> 00:48:44.030
We got people to do it again.

00:48:44.030 --> 00:48:47.070
If we got them legitimately
the first time.

00:48:47.070 --> 00:48:47.960
Yeah, and then if it wasn't good enough.

00:48:47.960 --> 00:48:50.927
And then we said, hey,
"We're gonna do that again,

00:48:50.927 --> 00:48:52.347
"now you know what's gonna happen,

00:48:52.347 --> 00:48:54.667
"and just really, like,
when you hear the dog bark,

00:48:54.667 --> 00:48:56.810
"just really react like you're scarred."

00:48:56.810 --> 00:48:58.380
And we were like, hopefully
enough of these people

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:01.200
